INKEY                                                    [2.40, 3.0]
-----

Format:      INKEY [string] %%envname [flags]

Purpose:     Reads a single keystroke and places it into an environ-
        ment variable.

Use:         When a  string is specified, it will be displayed. Next
        a  single keystroke  is read  and stored  in an  environment
        item, which name is specified by envname.

             Note that the environment item name must be preceded by
        two percent signs and not enclosed in percent signs.

             Normally any keystrokes already waiting in the keyboard
        buffer are  discarded first,  then INKEY  waits for  a  key,
        which is displayed and followed by a new line. This  can  be
        changed with the optional flags number (new in 3.0),  which
        is the sum of the following values:

        1    Do not wait for a key. If a keystroke is already  wait-
             ing in the keyboard buffer,  it is read and  displayed
             as usual; otherwise the environment item  is  deleted,
             so that %envname% expands to nothing,  and nothing  is
             displayed.

        2    Discard any keystrokes remaining in the keyboard buffer
             after the key has been read  (or after finding  no key
             waiting, with 1).

        4    Store the code of the key as two  hexadecimal  digits
             (for example 20 for the space bar,  1B for ESC, 09 for
             TAB, 08 for BS, 7F for DEL, 0D for RETURN) instead  of
             the key itself,  so that such keys can be tested.  The
             key is not displayed in this case, and neither is  the
             new line that normally follows it.

             So, for example, the flags value 0 (the default)  waits
        for a key, 1 only checks whether a key was pressed, 2 waits
        for a key and then empties the keyboard buffer,  3  checks
        for a key and then empties the keyboard buffer,  4 waits for
        a key and stores its code, and 7 does all three things.

Examples:

        INKEY %%KEY

             A keystroke  is read  and placed  into the  environment
        item KEY.

        INKEY Enter choice: %%KEY

             The  text "Enter choice: " is displayed and a keystroke
        is read and placed into the environment item KEY.

        INKEY %ITEM% %%KEY

             The value of the environment item ITEM is displayed and
        a keystroke  is read  and placed  into the  environment item
        KEY.

        INKEY %%KEY 1

             If a keystroke is waiting it is placed into KEY, other-
        wise KEY is deleted;  the batch file continues at once  ei-
        ther way.  This allows a .BTM batch file to check for a key
        press while it repeats some commands, for example:

                  ~LOOP
                  INKEY %%KEY 1
                  IF NOT {%KEY%} == {} GOTO ~DONE
                  ECHO Working...
                  GOTO ~LOOP
                  ~DONE
                  ECHO You pressed %KEY%

        INKEY Press any key %%KEY 2

             The text is displayed and a keystroke is read into KEY.
        Any other keystrokes typed meanwhile  (for example the  re-
        peats of a key held down)  are then discarded,  so that they
        do not reach the next command.

        INKEY Press ESC to cancel, any other key to continue %%KEY 4
        IF {%KEY%} == {1B} GOTO ~CANCEL

             The text is displayed and the code of the key  pressed
        is placed into KEY as two hexadecimal digits,  which is the
        only way to test for a key such as ESC.
